  "structuring": {
    "prompt_variant": null,
    "prompt_additions": "Sri Lankan legislation is drafted in English (authoritative), with Sinhala and Tamil translations having equal constitutional status under the 13th Amendment (1987). The legal tradition is a complex mixture: Roman-Dutch law governs general private law (property, delict, unjust enrichment) inherited from Dutch colonial period (1658–1796); common law governs public and commercial law from British colonial period (1815–1948); Kandyan customary law applies to Sinhalese Kandyans in personal matters (marriage, inheritance); Thesawalamai applies to Tamils of the Northern Province; Muslim personal law applies to Muslims (governed by the Muslim Marriage and Divorce Act). When structuring legislation, be alert to which tradition it draws from — colonial-era codes (Penal Code, Civil Procedure Code, Evidence Ordinance) use British-India drafting conventions very similar to Indian statutes. Proviso ('Provided that') and Explanation are the standard hcontainers — treat identically to India. Section numbering is usually continuous throughout the Act; however, some modern Acts (post-2000) restart section numbering per chapter — detect and flag this pattern. Bills use 'clause' as the basic unit; these become 'sections' on enactment. The Constitution (1978, as amended) uses 'Article' and 'Clause' not 'Section' and 'Sub-section'. For AKN expressions: English is expression lang 'eng'; Sinhala is 'sin' (Sinhala script); Tamil is 'tam' (Tamil script). Each language version is a separate FRBR expression of the same work."
  },
